WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.240
Auditory function is the brain's ability to use sound meaningfully.

00:00:04.835 --> 00:00:12.515
The ear and hearing technology deliver information, but meaningful listening depends on neural processing and experience.

00:00:13.275 --> 00:00:19.675
The lecture emphasizes early development because the brain is organizing its pathways rapidly during infancy.

00:00:20.545 --> 00:00:24.625
Neuroplasticity means that experience can change neural organization.

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:33.740
The course highlights greater auditory plasticity in the first three and a half years, without making that birthday an absolute end to learning.

00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:41.590
Cross-modal reorganization describes auditory areas being recruited for other sensory information when auditory input is absent.

00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:47.870
Developmental synchrony describes skills emerging near their expected developmental time.

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:53.860
These ideas explain the emphasis on early, consistent access and intervention.

00:00:54.595 --> 00:00:57.475
They do not mean that owning a device is enough.

00:00:58.045 --> 00:01:02.285
The signal must be usable, and the child needs meaningful language interaction.

